20:11:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: You can’t find the center of the universe because, according to our best understanding of cosmology, there isn’t one—not in the way we usually think of a center. Here’s why: 1. **The Big Bang wasn’t an explosion in space.** It was the sudden expansion of space itself. All of space was once extremely hot and dense, and then it started stretching. The expansion didn’t happen from a single point *into* an existing emptiness; instead, every point in the universe expanded away from every other point. There is no “special” location where the expansion started. 2. **Every observer sees themselves as the center of expansion.** Like dots on the surface of an inflating balloon: as the balloon stretches, every dot moves away from every other dot. From the perspective of any one dot, it looks like all the others are racing away, and it appears to be at the center. But no dot is truly central—the surface has no center. Our universe works similarly, but in three spatial dimensions instead of a 2D surface. 3. **The universe is (likely) infinite and uniform.** Observations suggest the universe is homogeneous and isotropic on large scales—it looks roughly the same in all directions from any location. If it’s infinite in extent, there is no boundary and no geometric center. Even if it’s finite but unbounded (like the 3D analogue of a sphere’s surface), it still has no center. So, the idea of a “centre of the universe” doesn’t apply. In a very real sense, **everywhere is the centre**—or, more accurately, the question itself assumes a perspective that doesn’t fit the geometry of our cosmos.
