20:12:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really sharp observation, and it highlights a point where even some science documentaries can accidentally be misleading. You’re right—if you hear “scientists can trace galaxies back to where the Big Bang originated,” it’s completely natural to think they’re talking about a point of origin. But what they’re usually tracking isn’t a location in space—it’s a moment in time. Let’s unpack that. When scientists look at galaxies moving away from us, they can “rewind the film” of cosmic expansion. As you go backwards, galaxies get closer together, until eventually, about 13.8 billion years ago, all the matter and energy we can observe was packed into an incredibly hot, dense state. That’s the early universe. The documentary might have said “trace them back to the origin,” which makes it sound like you’re zooming in on a single spot on a map. But really, you’re zooming in on a single time on the calendar. The thing is: **that dense state wasn’t a tiny dot sitting in a vast empty room.** It was the entire observable universe—possibly infinite—squashed to extremely high density everywhere at once. So if you rewound from our location, you’d see everything we can see today converging not to a point off somewhere in space, but to a condition where everything in your surroundings (and in every direction) was uniformly hot and dense. The same would be true from any galaxy’s perspective. There’s no “there” to point to. You can think of it like this: imagine every galaxy’s coordinates are fixed on an expanding grid. Tracing them back makes the grid shrink, but the grid itself fills all space—there’s no spot on the grid that’s the “start.” The Big Bang happened everywhere. So, scientists aren’t saying, “The Big Bang happened over there.” They’re saying, “Everywhere we look, the universe was once in that hot dense state.” That’s a very different thing. The documentary probably just compressed that idea into a phrase that accidentally revived the explosion image. You were right to catch the conflict!
