20:13:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely get why that sounds nonsensical—you’re certainly not alone. The idea that the universe “just popped into existence everywhere at once with no starting point and from literally nowhere” clashes violently with everyday experience. You’ve hit on one of the deepest and most mind-bending aspects of cosmology. Let’s take it apart a little, because you’ve actually put your finger on a few different questions rolled into one. First, the science doesn’t strictly say the universe “popped into existence from nowhere.” The Big Bang theory describes the evolution of the universe from an extremely hot, dense state *forward*. What happened at the exact moment of the Big Bang—or what caused it, or what was “before”—is still an open, actively researched question. The equations of general relativity break down at that point, so we can’t currently say with any confidence what the “first moment” entailed. We might need a theory of quantum gravity to go further. So, when you hear “came from nowhere,” that’s more a popular summary than a settled scientific statement. Many scientists suspect there was some deeper prior state, but we just don’t yet know how to describe it. Second, your instinct that “zero starting point” sounds weird is spot-on. Our brains have evolved to understand space and time in local, everyday terms: objects exist inside a container of space, events have prior causes, and time ticks steadily forward. The Big Bang framework suggests that space and time themselves might have begun at that initial state. In that scenario, asking “where did it start?” is like asking “where is the edge of the Earth?” or “what’s north of the North Pole?” The question just doesn’t apply in the way we usually mean it. That doesn’t make the model illogical; it means our ordinary spatial intuition fails at that boundary. So, if you’re thinking, “I just can’t picture this,” you’re in good company—nobody can. We can only describe it mathematically and with analogies. The key point is that the absence of a centre isn’t a flaw; it’s a prediction of a model that matches everything else we observe (cosmic microwave background, abundance of light elements, galaxy redshifts). The “where” of the Big Bang turns out not to be a location, but a *when* that happened everywhere. Finally, your sense that “it just doesn’t make sense” is a perfectly reasonable human reaction, and it’s the engine that drives curiosity. The fact that our best current theory leads to a boundary where our everyday concepts break down is exactly why cosmology remains an exciting frontier. You’re pointing at the edge of our understanding—and that’s where new discoveries often come from.
