20:14:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s such an important question, and I really appreciate you asking it. If the way you’ve worded it reflects a bit of frustration, that’s completely understandable. You’re right: many of us were taught exactly that—the Big Bang started from a single, tiny point, and everything exploded outward. So when you hear “actually, that’s not right,” it feels like the ground shifts. Let’s look at why that “single point” story is so common, and where it goes wrong. **1. The misconception is baked into a lot of early science education.** When teachers first introduce the Big Bang, they often compress a very complex idea into a simple, visceral picture: “The whole universe was squashed into a dot smaller than an atom, and then it burst outward.” That image is easy to draw and easy to remember. The problem is, it’s a brutal oversimplification that smuggles in the very thing we’re trying to avoid—a pre-existing space where the dot sits. Elementary school textbooks rarely later circle back to say, “By the way, that dot was actually everywhere, not a location in space.” So generations of students grow up with a mental picture that’s physically incorrect. **2. The confusion often comes from mixing up the *observable* universe with the *whole* universe.** The part of the universe we can see today—our observable universe—was once contained in a very small, hot, dense region. We can trace it back to a tiny volume. So scientists do sometimes say, “The observable universe was once the size of a grapefruit.” That’s true. But that grapefruit-sized region wasn’t hanging in a void—it was just the patch of the larger universe that we can now see. The whole universe, if it’s infinite now, was already infinite then, just denser. The “grapefruit” wasn’t a single point; it was our entire visible bubble squeezed down small, still surrounded by more universe beyond it. Textbooks often skip that distinction. **3. The “explosion from a point” idea is a hangover from older, less precise analogies.** In the mid-20th century, when the Big Bang theory was being popularised, many descriptions literally called it a “cosmic explosion” or a “primeval atom.” That language stuck. Even today, documentary scripts sometimes revert to it for simplicity, even if the scientists they interview know better. So what “everyone learns” is often a cultural shorthand, not the careful scientific model. **4. It’s not that everything you learned is totally wrong—it’s that it’s incomplete.** The core idea—that the universe expanded from an incredibly hot, dense early state—is still correct. What’s being refined is the “where” and “how” of that expansion. The shift is from “a single point in a pre-existing emptiness” to “every-where expanding everywhere.” That’s the part that early education tends to botch. So, how can this be “totally wrong” yet still what everyone learns? The same way kids learn that electrons orbit the nucleus like tiny planets, and only later learn about probability clouds. Or that dinosaurs were all scaly lizards, and then we discovered many had feathers. Education often layers understanding: simple, intuitive pictures first, then more accurate ones later. The trouble is, for the Big Bang, many people never get the later layer unless they specifically study it. What you’re experiencing now isn’t that you were duped—it’s that you’re moving past the simplified version into the real, weird, wonderful truth. And that can feel like a betrayal. But it’s actually a sign you’re thinking at a deeper level than the bedtime-story version allowed. You’re not wrong to feel that the old picture was misleading; it was. But now you’re seeing the cosmos as it really is: without a center, without an edge, and far stranger and more beautiful than any simple explosion.
