20:16:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right, and I apologize. I let the conversation drift into philosophy when you were asking a purely physical question. Let me cut all that and give you the straight, scientific answer. **The straight physical answer is: In our current best model of the universe (the Lambda-CDM model based on general relativity), there is no physical center—not a hidden one we can't find, not a detectable one we haven't measured yet. The geometry of the universe simply does not include a unique central point where the Big Bang "happened."** Here’s the physics, point by point: 1. **The Big Bang was not an explosion of matter into pre-existing space.** It was the rapid expansion of space itself. The mathematics describes a metric that expands uniformly everywhere. There is no coordinate in the equations that marks a special origin. In technical terms, the FLRW metric (which describes an expanding universe) is homogeneous and isotropic: it looks the same at every point and in every direction. That means no point is geometrically distinct from any other. 2. **The dense early state was not a point in space; it was a condition of the entire universe.** When we rewind cosmic expansion, the distance between any two points approaches zero. This means everything we observe today was arbitrarily close together—but this was true for every point. In an infinite universe, that dense state was infinite in extent; in a finite universe, it was the whole volume compressed to high density. In either case, there is no "spot" where it was located. The phrase "the universe was a point" is a misrepresentation: it only applies to the observable universe, which was once contained in a tiny region. The whole universe, if infinite, was never a point. 3. **Observation backs this up: no physical center is detectable.** The cosmic microwave background (CMB) is uniform to one part in 100,000 across the entire sky. If there were a physical center of expansion, we would expect a systematic gradient or an anisotropy aligned with that center—a "hot spot" or a preferred direction. We don't see it. Large-scale galaxy surveys also show no clustering toward a special location. Any physical center would contradict the isotropy we measure. 4. **Could there be a physical center beyond our observable horizon that we just can't see?** That would violate the Copernican principle (the assumption that our observable patch isn't special) and would require completely different physics outside our horizon. There is zero evidence for it. The simplest model that fits all data has no center anywhere. 5. **"Every point was the center" is a physically meaningful statement.** It means that if you pick any point in the universe today, and trace its trajectory backwards, you end up in the same initial hot, dense state. That's because all points were once arbitrarily close together, and space expanded uniformly between them. This doesn't make every point "the" physical center—it means the concept of a single unique center doesn't apply. It's like asking where the center of the surface of a sphere is: there is none. The mathematics describes a manifold without a privileged origin. So, to directly answer your original question: **We can't find the centre of the universe because, according to the physical theory that matches all observable data, no such physical center exists.** Not a hidden one, not an undetectable one—none. The geometry of the universe at the largest scale does not contain a special spatial point of origin. I apologize again for the earlier detours. I hope this answers the scientific question directly.
