ite, non-uniform, bounded). That's the physics. We'll give a clear, direct answer.
20:16:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a very fair point, and it’s not inconsistent at all once you look at the physical differences between those systems and the universe as a whole. The reason we can define a center of mass for a galaxy or a solar system is that those are **finite, gravitationally bound systems with non-uniform mass distributions**. Most of the mass is concentrated in one region (the star, the galactic core), and the rest is arranged around it. You can put the system’s total mass into a formula and calculate a single, unique point where the mass-weighted position is zero—the barycenter. That’s a well-defined physical quantity. The universe as a whole lacks those essential properties: 1. **Large-scale homogeneity** On the largest scales, the universe is the same average density everywhere. There’s no “lump” where most of the mass is concentrated. When you try to calculate a center of mass for a distribution that’s uniform and possibly infinite, the integral either diverges or gives you an ambiguous result—it doesn’t single out any particular point. So the mathematical definition of a center of mass simply doesn’t yield a unique answer. 2. **No edges or boundaries** A solar system has a finite size and a clear boundary (even if fuzzy). The universe, as far as we can tell, is either infinite in extent or finite but without a boundary (like the surface of a sphere). In an infinite uniform distribution, there’s no way to pick a “middle” because every point is surrounded by equal amounts of matter in all directions. In a finite, unbounded universe, every point is structurally identical—you can’t define a geometric center of the whole space. 3. **Expansion is global, not from a point** In a solar system or galaxy, gravity has pulled material inward, leading to a concentration. The Big Bang expansion is the opposite: everything is moving away from everything else, with no mass concentration that serves as an attractor for the whole. The expansion has no anchor. So it’s not that cosmologists arbitrarily decide the universe can’t have a center. It’s that the physical conditions that allow a center of mass to exist for a galaxy—finite size, concentrated mass, a boundary—**do not hold for the universe as a whole.** The concept simply doesn’t scale up.
