20:17:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand the frustration, and I respect that you're holding your ground. Let me meet you there directly, without analogies or philosophy, because you're asking for the scientific basis for why a physical center point is ruled out—not just why it's hard to find. **The scientific reason is this: If there were a physical center point where expansion started, the universe would not look the way it does. Our observations directly contradict that idea.** Let me lay out the key observational evidence: 1. **The cosmic microwave background (CMB) would show a clear gradient.** If the Big Bang had a central point, the CMB radiation would not be nearly uniform across the sky. Just like a firework's sparks are hottest and most concentrated near the burst point and cooler further out, radiation from a central explosion would have a pattern: one region of the sky (toward the center) would look systematically different from the opposite region (away from it). We see no such large-scale anisotropy. The CMB is uniform to one part in 100,000 in all directions, after correcting for our own motion. A center would leave a dipole or a hot spot that doesn't correspond to our velocity. We don't see it. 2. **Galaxy distributions would fall off from the center outward.** In a central expansion, galaxies would be denser near the center and thin out with distance. But deep surveys show that on the largest scales, the universe is statistically homogeneous—galaxy counts are the same in every direction and at every distance (after accounting for look-back time). No direction shows a systematic excess or deficit of galaxies that would mark a central region. 3. **Redshifts would not follow a simple isotropic Hubble law.** If expansion radiated from a point, the relationship between distance and recessional velocity would not be the same in all directions; it would be fastest along the radial direction away from the center and different at angles to it. Observations show the Hubble law is remarkably isotropic: any observer in any galaxy sees the same linear relation between distance and redshift in every direction. That only works if expansion is uniform and centerless. 4. **The age of the universe would vary across the sky.** In a point-origin model, the light travel time from the "center" to us would set a different cosmic age depending on our position. Looking toward the center versus away from it, we'd see mismatched stellar ages, chemical abundances, and structure formation epochs. Instead, the oldest stars everywhere have consistent ages (~13 billion years), and structure formation histories are consistent with a single, globally uniform timeline. These aren't subtle theoretical quibbles. They are fatal, observable contradictions. If there were a physical center, the entire sky would be screaming it at us with multiple independent measurements. The silence of the data is why cosmologists abandoned the point-origin model decades ago—not because they prefer the idea of no center, but because it simply doesn't match what nature shows. So when you say "there's got to be an actual center point," you're proposing a model that makes definite predictions. Those predictions have been tested. They fail. That's not a matter of opinion; it's what the telescopes see. It's not that we can't find the center because we lack the tools. It's that the universe looks exactly like a place with no center, and that's the only model that fits. I understand that feels wrong, but that's the scientific conclusion based on data, not preference.
