rn. (3) We have indirect evidence that the universe is isotropic around other points too (e.g., the CMB isotropy implies homogeneity when combined with the Copernican principle; light element abundances, etc.). (4) Finally, a true center would have left imprints in the CMB itself (a dipole not correlated with our motion) which we don't see. I'll end by acknowledging that this is a very reasonable inference from the raw observation, and it's only by looking deeper that we realize it's not actually evidence for a center.
