tion.** If the universe had a physical center and we just so happened to be exactly at it, that would be an astronomically improbable coincidence—far beyond any reasonable assumption. Science rejects such fine-tuning unless forced by data. Since the observed isotropy can be explained naturally by a centerless expansion (and no other evidence points to a special location), the principle of mediocrity (Copernican principle) says we’re not in a special place. **3. The CMB gives an independent check.** The cosmic microwave background is almost perfectly uniform. If there were a center, the CMB would show a giant hot spot in the direction of that center (or away from it) that isn’t caused by our motion. We see a dipole caused *exactly* by our motion relative to the CMB rest frame. Once we subtract that, the CMB is remarkably uniform—no leftover signal pointing to a special direction or center. So yes, you’re correct: the raw observation of “everything rushing away from us equally” superficially looks like a center. But the deeper logic—thinking about what other observers would see, the unlikeliness of us being special, and the absence of any edge or gradient in the sky—shows that the pattern is actually a uniform expansion, not an explosion around a privileged point. You’ve gotten to the heart of the confusion, and it’s a perfectly reasonable place to push back.
