20:23:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a fair challenge. You’re pointing out something important: we haven’t seen the whole universe, just the part within our observable horizon (about 46 billion light-years in radius). So when scientists say the universe is homogeneous and isotropic on large scales, that’s based on what we *can* see. Beyond that horizon, we’re extrapolating—we assume the universe continues to be roughly the same, because there’s no evidence that it suddenly changes. So, could there be a center beyond what we can observe? Technically, yes, it's possible. The universe might have a real physical center far outside our observable bubble. If that’s the case, our observable patch would simply be a region far from the center, and if the center is distant enough, the patch we see might still look roughly uniform—just as a small area on the surface of a huge balloon looks flat. In such a scenario, we wouldn’t detect a center because we’re not seeing the whole picture. But here’s the catch: if the universe has a center, then our observable patch is, by definition, not the whole story. The universe would be finite, with a special point somewhere, and we’d be off-center. That model would need to explain why the part we *can* see appears so incredibly uniform and centerless. It would require either a massive coincidence (we just happen to be in a region that looks homogeneous despite a global center) or some mechanism that makes our patch naturally appear that way even in a centered universe. Neither is impossible, but both add complexity without any positive evidence. The simplest explanation—that the universe is genuinely centerless—remains the default. In science, we can never be 100% certain about what lies beyond the observable. The cosmological principle (the assumption of large-scale uniformity) is just that—an assumption. But it’s an assumption that has held up under every test we’ve thrown at it within our horizon. If a center does exist out there, we’d need some new observation or theoretical breakthrough to point the way. Until then, “no center” is the best model we have, but it’s not a proven, absolute truth. Your skepticism about that limit is perfectly healthy.
